## A. The framework and the replication

**Q1. Why does Z_SB grow with N_rand and not with the size of the cuisine?**
Because Z_SB = √N_rand · (mean Ns_cuisine − mean Ns_rand) / σ_rand, where σ_rand is the standard deviation of recipe-level Ns in the null cuisine, not the standard error of the cuisine's mean. The cuisine's own size never enters; only N_rand does. So Z_SB = √N_rand · d, with d = ΔNs/σ_rand a standardised effect size. With N_rand = {{ n0(M.n_rand) }}, √N_rand = {{ f(M.sqrt_n_rand, 1) }}: Korea has d = {{ f(R.korea_d, 3) }} and Z = {{ f(R.korea_z, 1) }} (ratio {{ f(R.z_over_d_korea, 1) }}, rounding); across all regions Z/d lies between {{ f(R.z_over_d_min, 1) }} and {{ f(R.z_over_d_max, 1) }}. Consequences: Z_SB is an effect size ×{{ f(M.sqrt_n_rand, 0) }}, not a significance statistic; any cuisine would become "more significant" just by drawing a larger null. That is why we report d with bootstrap CIs, size-aware p-values (null distribution of the mean at the cuisine's own N), and compare models at equal N.

**Q2. Your Korea Z is {{ f(R.korea_z, 1) }}; we published {{ R.korea_z_published }}. Why the difference?**
The 2018 FlavorDB snapshot used in the paper is no longer online; both FlavorDB endpoints now serve current data with updated molecule lists, and the published targets were read by eye from the site figures. With current FlavorDB, contrasting cuisines come out somewhat more contrasting, but signs ({{ R.random.sign_agreement }}/{{ R.random.n }}) and ranks (Spearman ρ = {{ f(R.random.spearman, 3) }}) are preserved. {% set dz = (R.korea_z - R.korea_z_published)|abs %}In effect-size terms the gap is small: {{ f(dz, 1) }} in Z is {{ f(dz / M.sqrt_n_rand, 3) }} in d. A molecule-dropout robustness analysis is planned {{ pending("not yet run") }}.

**Q3. Korea's Soybean Sauce contributes {{ f(R.korea_top1_ours) }}% in your run but {{ R.korea_top1_published }}% in ours. Is the contribution replicated?**
The ranking is: Soybean Sauce is Korea's #1 contributor in both, and our top-3 is {{ join_and(R.korea_top3|map(attribute='name')|list) }}, as published. The paper does not specify the exact removal procedure, so we calibrated it against the published top contributors: removing the ingredient from its recipes and measuring the % change in the cuisine's mean Ns (oriented by the sign) reproduced the published rankings best (details in `docs/replication_report.md`). We report rankings as the replicated quantity and magnitudes as indicative.

**Q4. How did you treat the 24 CulinaryDB ingredients without FlavorDB profiles, and additives like salt and water?**
Exactly as the paper describes: 13 coarse-grained items inherit their FlavorDB parent's profile, Cayenne, Yeast, Tequila and Sauerkraut come from Ahn et al. 2011 via CAS → PubChem, MSG and citric acid are their molecules, and Baking Powder, Cooking Spray, Gelatin, Food Coloring and Liquid Smoke have no molecules. In a calibration grid, keeping empty-profile ingredients inside recipes (they add zero-sharing pairs) fitted the published Z best, and removing the Additive category broke the replication, so salt, sugar and water are part of the published analysis (`docs/replication_report.md`).

## B. The sample-size finding and the equal-N correction

**Q5. Why does the own-pool null depend on sample size?**
Each null draws ingredients from the sample's own ingredient pool. A small sample loses the cuisine's long tail of rare ingredients, which tend to share few compounds; the remaining pool is richer in frequent, high-sharing ingredients, so the null's mean Ns rises while the real recipes' mean Ns does not. ΔNs therefore falls as N shrinks. USA at {{ E.n_sub }} recipes: the pool shrinks to {{ n0(E.usa.pool_n160) }} ingredients and ΔNs = {{ f(E.usa.delta_ns_own_pool_n160) }}; the same samples against the full cuisine's pool give {{ f(E.usa.delta_ns_full_pool_n160) }} (Backup B1, F2d).

**Q6. How did you correct for it?**
With an equal-N reference: {{ n0(E.n_genuine_per_region) }} random {{ E.n_sub }}-recipe samples of every real cuisine, scored by the same function as the LLM cuisines (`flavoraudit.audit.scorecard_row`). Each LLM cuisine's effect size d is placed in the distribution of d over genuine samples of the same region and size: empirical two-sided p, BH across regions, "too uniform" above the 97.5% quantile and "too contrasting" below the 2.5% quantile. The pre-registered rule (LLM ΔNs CI vs the full cuisine's sign) is still reported, together with its false-positive rate. Why {{ E.n_sub }}: the generation design gives {{ D.n_conditions }} prompts × {{ D.calls_per_cell }} calls × {{ D.recipes_per_call }} recipes = {{ D.recipes_per_region }} recipes per model and region, the same for every model. The amendment was written into the decision log before the full LLM analysis, and it follows from the genuine-sample calibration, not from LLM results.

**Q7. Does this undermine Singh & Bagler's Fig. 4?**
Not the contrasting pattern. Cut to {{ E.matched_n }} recipes, all {{ E.contrasting_robust }} contrasting cuisines are negative in every sample. {{ R.contrasting_in_smallest7 }} of the {{ F.regions.n_contrasting }} contrasting cuisines are among CulinaryDB's 7 smallest, which would bias them toward "contrasting", but they stay contrasting at equal size, so the pattern is real. For uniform cuisines, {{ E.uniform_robust }}/{{ F.regions.n_uniform }} stay positive in ≥ 95% of samples, {{ join_and(E.uniform_weakened) }} weaken, and {{ join_and(E.uniform_flipped) }} flip ({% for r in E.uniform_flipped %}{{ r }} {{ pct(E.share[r], 0) }} positive{{ ", " if not loop.last }}{% endfor %}): their published positive sign relies on their larger ingredient pool.

**Q8. How often does your pre-registered sign-error rule misfire on real recipes?**
On genuine {{ E.n_sub }}-recipe samples it fires {{ pct(E.fpr) }} of the time overall, and {% for r, v in E.fpr_top %}{{ r }} {{ pct(v, 0) }}{{ ", " if not loop.last }}{% endfor %} for the worst regions. Genuine samples of that size agree with the full-cuisine sign only {{ pct(E.genuine_sign_agreement, 0) }} of the time on average. That is why the rule is reported but is not our primary inference.


## C. LLM results

**Q9. Do LLMs push cuisines toward uniform pairing (H1)?**
No common drift. Equal-N Wilcoxon test of a shift toward uniform: {% for k, p in L.items() %}{{ p.name }} median shift {{ sf(p.equal_n_median_shift_d) }} (p_adj {{ pv(p.h1eq_wilcoxon.p_adj) }}){{ "; " if not loop.last else "." }}{% endfor %} The pre-registered Fisher test (errors cluster in contrasting regions) is supported for {{ V.h1_fisher_supported }}/{{ V.n }} models. Errors are model-specific: {% for k, p in L.items() %}{{ p.name }} {{ p.equal_n_typical }} typical / {{ p.equal_n_too_uniform }} too uniform / {{ p.equal_n_too_contrasting }} too contrasting{{ "; " if not loop.last else "." }}{% endfor %} Consistent across every model with data: {% for c in F.consensus %}{{ c.region }} {{ c.verdict }} ({{ c.k }}/{{ c.k }}){{ ", " if not loop.last else "." }}{% endfor %} Note that the realistic ceiling for sign agreement is not 1 but the genuine-sample agreement of {{ f(E.genuine_sign_agreement) }} at the same N (LLMs: {{ rng(rs.sign_agreement) }}).


**Q10. Do LLMs amplify stereotypes (H2b)?**
Not on average: the median SAI is {{ rng(rs.median_sai) }} (genuine 95% range {{ f(E.sai_band[0], 3) }}–{{ f(E.sai_band[1], 3) }}) and the one-sided test is significant for {{ V.h2b_sai_supported }}/{{ V.n }} models. Per region, SAI deviates in both directions (F8). What LLMs do is narrow the cuisine: diversity ratio {{ rng(rs.median_diversity_ratio) }} (significant in {{ V.h2b_div_supported }}/{{ V.n }}) and prevalence JSD {{ rng(rs.median_prevalence_jsd, 3) }} vs {{ f(E.ref.prevalence_jsd, 3) }} for genuine samples.

**Q11. Why is the genuine fingerprint correlation only {{ f(E.ref.fingerprint_spearman) }}, not 1?**
Category fingerprints (ΔNs per randomised category, Jain et al. 2015) are noisy at small N. That is why H2c compares LLM fingerprints with the resampling distribution of genuine samples, not with 1. LLM fingerprints reach {{ rng(rs.median_fingerprint_spearman) }}; H2c is supported for {{ V.h2c_supported }}/{{ V.n }} models.

**Q12. Do LLM cuisines rely on the same flavor drivers (RQ2a)?**
{% set DR = F.drivers %}{% if DR %}Only partly. For each region we compare the top-5 contributors (χ%, % change in mean Ns on removal) of the LLM cuisine with the real cuisine's: median Jaccard@5 {% for k in DR.order %}{{ DR.models[k].name }} {{ f(DR.models[k].median_jaccard5) }} (rank correlation {{ f(DR.models[k].median_spearman) }}, {{ DR.models[k].regions }} regions){{ "; " if not loop.last }}{% endfor %}, against {{ f(DR.real.median_jaccard5) }} (rank correlation {{ f(DR.real.median_spearman) }}) for genuine {{ E.n_sub }}-recipe samples. For Korea, the real top-5 is {{ join_and(DR.korea_real_top5) }}; {{ join_and(DR.korea_match) }} put {{ DR.korea_real_top5[0] }} first{% if DR.korea_miss %}, while {% for k in DR.order if DR.models[k].name in DR.korea_miss %}{{ DR.models[k].name }} leads with {{ DR.models[k].korea_first }}{{ ", " if not loop.last }}{% endfor %}{% endif %}. H2a (drivers differ) is therefore supported in the sense that no model reaches the genuine-sample overlap.{% else %}{{ pending("drivers not yet in results/summary.json") }}{% endif %}

**Q13. How did you pick the real and LLM recipes on slide 9? Isn't that cherry-picking?**
{% set P = F.extras.pair %}{% if P %}By a rule fixed before looking: in {{ P.region }} (the lab's showcase contrasting cuisine), the real CulinaryDB recipe whose Ns is closest to the median Ns of all {{ P.real.pool }} real {{ P.region }} recipes ({{ f(P.real.median_ns, 1) }}), and the LLM recipe closest to the median of all {{ P.llm.pool }} plain-prompt recipes of the complete models ({{ f(P.llm.median_ns, 1) }}); ties go to the lowest id. That gave {{ P.real.title }} ({{ P.real.source }}) and {{ P.llm.title }} ({{ P.llm.model }}). They are the typical recipes of each source, not the best or worst.{% else %}{{ pending("recipe pair unavailable") }}{% endif %}

