**Q17. Why is copy-mutate a baseline and not a contribution?**
It is the lab's own model (Kinouchi et al. 2008; Jain & Bagler 2018; Tuwani et al. 2019, Algorithm 1 with ranked fitness), and our Assignment 3. It answers "what does a simple cultural-evolution model reproduce from the region's own ingredients and their popularity?". It reproduces the popularity curve (rank-frequency MAE {{ f(cm.freq_rank_mae, 3) }}) but not composition (JSD {{ f(cm.prevalence_jsd) }}), signatures (SAI {{ f(cm.sai) }}) or fingerprints (ρ {{ f(cm.fingerprint_spearman) }}), and gets the pairing sign right in {{ pct(cm.sign_match, 0) }} of regions. LLMs beat it on prevalence ({{ V.cm_better.prevalence_jsd }}/{{ V.n }}), fingerprint ({{ V.cm_better.fingerprint_spearman }}/{{ V.n }}) and SAI ({{ V.cm_better.abs_log_sai }}/{{ V.n }}), but on the pairing gap only {{ join_and(F.cm_gap_models) }}.

**Q18. Why these models, and not bigger ones?**
The proposal promises ≥ {{ D.proposal_min_models }} LLMs with ≥ 1 proprietary and ≥ {{ D.proposal_min_open_families }} open-weight families; no model count was set by the course. We used free open-weight models on Kaggle GPUs (Qwen3.5-9B, Llama-3.1-8B) and two inexpensive proprietary APIs (Gemini 3.5 Flash-Lite on the free tier, GPT-6-Luna via the Batch API), {{ ds.n_models }} models from {{ families|length }} families so far. Larger models were configured but not run, to put the effort into the audit and steering. The sampling policy was fixed before any data: open weights at T = 0.7, top-p = 0.95, thinking off (developer recommendations range from near-greedy to 0.7, and near-greedy decoding gives near-duplicate recipes); proprietary APIs at provider defaults with the lowest reasoning level. A T = 1.0 sensitivity subset is pre-registered {{ pending("not yet run") }}.


## D. Aliasing and data

**Q19. How was the aliasing validated?**
We reconstructed CulinaryDB's protocol (lower-case, singularise, remove culinary stop-words, longest n-gram match against entity names and synonyms, tie-breaks learned from expert lines) and evaluated it on CulinaryDB's own raw ingredient lines, which carry the expert mapping. On {{ n0(AL.test_lines) }} held-out lines it agrees on {{ pct(AL.acc_lines) }} of unique lines and {{ pct(AL.acc_rows) }} of occurrences, with {{ pct(AL.coverage, 2) }} coverage. On LLM text, {{ pct_rng(ds.alias_full_min, ds.alias_full_max) }} of lines are fully matched; unmatched terms (e.g. "gochugaru") go to a curation list that a human accepts or rejects {{ pending("curation not yet applied") if ds.curation_pending else "(applied)" }}. We deliberately did not let an LLM do the mapping: CulinaryDB's human lines were mapped with this protocol, so LLM lines must be too, or the mapper's own biases would enter exactly the quantities we measure.


## E. Steering (D3)

**Q20. Why is the scorer's classifier linear?**
Because its per-ingredient contributions are the feedback: they name the "atypical" ingredients in critique-and-revise and in the app's suggestions. Among linear models the {{ SC.classifier_name }} won on validation (macro-F1 {{ f(SC.val_macro_f1_selected, 3) }}); gradient boosting was slightly better ({{ f(SC.val_macro_f1_gbdt, 3) }}) but not interpretable, so it became the independent evaluation classifier instead. Temperature scaling lowers the calibration error (ECE) from {{ f(SC.ece_raw) }} to {{ f(SC.ece_calibrated) }}.

**Q21. A macro-F1 of {{ f(SC.test_macro_f1) }} looks low. Is the scorer useful?**
It is a {{ F.regions.n }}-class problem (chance ≈ {{ f(SC.chance, 3) }}); top-3 accuracy is {{ f(SC.test_top3) }}. Distinctive cuisines are easy ({% for r, v in SC.f1_best %}{{ r }} {{ f(v) }}{{ ", " if not loop.last }}{% endfor %}), overlapping Western ones hard ({% for r, v in SC.f1_worst %}{{ r }} {{ f(v) }}{{ ", " if not loop.last }}{% endfor %}). The composite score, not the classifier alone, is what steers: its test AUC for real held-out recipes vs other-cuisine / copy-mutate / null recipes is {{ f(SC.auc_test.other_cuisines.composite) }} / {{ f(SC.auc_test.copy_mutate.composite) }} / {{ f(SC.auc_test.random_null.composite) }}, because the classifier separates other cuisines while co-occurrence separates copy-mutate and null recipes.

**Q22. How does steering avoid rewarding copying?**
Three guards. (1) Admissibility: a candidate that is a near-copy of any CulinaryDB recipe (max Jaccard ≥ {{ M.near_copy }}) or duplicates an already selected ingredient set is never selected; the selected near-copy rate is {{ f(B.near_copy_max) if B else pending("results") }}. (2) Held-out evaluation: the scorer is never the yardstick; we report an independent classifier (different model class, disjoint training split), prevalence JSD, SAI, fingerprint, pairing sign, diversity and novelty. (3) Ablations: each scorer component dropped in turn (F15b).

**Q23. Did steering work?**
{% if B %}Best-of-N: the independent classifier's share rises with N for {{ "every model" if B.all_acc_up else "some models" }} ({% for k in B.order %}{% set r = B.rows[k] %}{{ r.name }} {{ f(r.first.indep_classifier_acc) }} → {{ f(r.last.indep_classifier_acc) }}{{ "; " if not loop.last }}{% endfor %}) while diversity falls ({% for k in B.order %}{% set r = B.rows[k] %}{{ r.name }} {{ f(r.first.diversity_ratio) }} → {{ f(r.last.diversity_ratio) }}{{ "; " if not loop.last }}{% endfor %}); F15 also shows SAI and similarity to CulinaryDB rising, and cuisine-level sign, fingerprint and JSD do not consistently improve at {{ B.meta.selected_max }} recipes per region. Selection trades diversity for recognisability. {% endif %}{% if RV %}Critique-and-revise does better: classifier share {% for k in RV.order %}{% set r = RV.rows[k] %}{{ r.name }} {{ f(r.first.indep_classifier_acc) }} → {{ f(r.last.indep_classifier_acc) }} ({{ r.rounds }} round{{ "s" if r.rounds > 1 }}){{ "; " if not loop.last }}{% endfor %}, prevalence JSD lower in {{ RV.improved.prevalence_jsd }}/{{ RV.n_models }} models, fingerprint ρ higher in {{ RV.improved.fingerprint_spearman }}/{{ RV.n_models }}, while revised recipes keep a Jaccard overlap of {{ rng_text(RV.kept_min, RV.kept_max) }} with the original. No CIs yet, so this is promising, not conclusive.{% else %}Critique-and-revise: {{ pending("running") }}.{% endif %}

**Q24. In your steering example the independent classifier still says USA. Did steering work?**
{% set X2 = steering_example %}{% if X2 %}Modestly, and we say so. The example was picked by a rule fixed in advance ({{ X2.rule }}), so it is a typical case: the feedback named missing signature ingredients that pair well ({{ join_and((X2.feedback_given or {}).missing or []) }}), the model added exactly those, and the fidelity percentile rose {{ f(X2.before.fidelity_percentile, 1) }} → {{ f(X2.after.fidelity_percentile, 1) }}, but the independent classifier assigns it to {{ X2.after.independent_classifier }} before and after. Across all {{ X2.context.revisions_scored }} round-1 revisions the score rose in {{ pct(X2.context.share_score_raised, 0) }}, and the median percentile went from {{ f(X2.context.median_percentile_before, 1) }} to {{ f(X2.context.median_percentile_after, 1) }}. At the cuisine level, critique-and-revise raised the independent classifier's share and lowered prevalence JSD (slide 16), without CIs yet.{% else %}{{ pending("steering example unavailable") }}{% endif %}

**Q25. Why did best-of-N rerank existing samples instead of generating the pre-registered {{ n0(SD.pool_target) }}-candidate pool?**
Compute. The pool needs {{ n0(SD.extra_calls_per_model) }} extra calls per model; at the measured rate of the Qwen full run on Kaggle ({{ f(F.resources.kaggle_minutes, 1) }} min for {{ D.planned_calls }} calls, i.e. {{ f(SD.minutes_per_call, 2) }} min per call on the T4 "{{ F.resources.kaggle_engine }}") that is ≈ {{ f(SD.gpu_hours_per_model, 0) }} GPU-hours per model{% if SD.gpu_hours_min %} ({{ f(SD.gpu_hours_min, 0) }}–{{ f(SD.gpu_hours_max, 0) }} across the Kaggle full runs: {% for n, r in SD.run_rates.items() %}{{ n }} {{ f(r.minutes, 1) }} min{{ ", " if not loop.last }}{% endfor %}){% endif %}. We therefore reranked the {{ B.meta.pool_max if B else "?" }} recipes per region the models had already written, with {{ B.meta.selected_max if B else "?" }} picks per N, and documented the deviation. Consequence: cuisine-level metrics at that size are noisy, which we state as a limitation.

**Q26. Isn't the independent classifier circular with the scorer's classifier?**
Partly. It is a different model class ({{ SC.eval_model }}) trained on a disjoint split, but both learn ingredient → cuisine, and F15b shows the best-of-N gain shrinks when the scorer's classifier component is dropped. That is why we also report non-classifier held-out metrics (JSD, fingerprint, sign), and why we read best-of-N as a trade-off rather than a success.

## F. The project

**Q27. How is this different from Group 23?**
Group 23's proposal (per our project brief) covers 8 cuisines with an Indian-regional focus and about 2,400 recipes, with same-dish LLM/human pairs and an LLM-vs-human classifier. We cover all {{ F.regions.n }} CulinaryDB regions with {{ n0(ds.total_recipes) }} recipes so far (design target ≥ {{ n0(D.proposal_min_recipes) }}), {{ ds.n_models }} LLMs from {{ families|length }} families, a copy-mutate evolutionary baseline, the equal-N reference, and fingerprint-guided steering. We deliberately avoided an Indian-regional focus, same-dish pairs and an LLM-vs-human detector.

**Q28. Can someone reproduce this?**
Yes, by scripts: fixed seeds; frozen prompts (prompt set {{ D.prompt_version }}, hashed); every LLM call logged as JSONL (prompt, model id, parameters, usage, raw output); `analysis/summarize.py` writes `results/summary.json`, and `presentation/build.py` regenerates these slides, the poster and these notes from it. The library has {{ F.library.n_tests }} tests (including a slow regression that reproduces the Fig. 4 signs) and CI on Python {{ join_and(F.library.ci_python) }}.

**Q29. Licences, cost and AI assistance?**
CulinaryDB and FlavorDB are CC BY-NC-SA 3.0, so derived data (LLMCuisine-22, flavor matrices, app artifacts) are released under CC BY-NC-SA 4.0 with attribution; the Ahn et al. data are CC BY 4.0; our code is MIT, and the data are downloaded on first use rather than bundled in the MIT package. LLM output terms (Llama 3.1 "Built with Llama", Qwen Apache-2.0, OpenAI and Gemini terms) are recorded in the datasheet. Cash spent: ${{ f(F.resources.openai_usd) }} on OpenAI (budget cap ${{ F.resources.budget_usd }}); Gemini ran on the free tier and the open models on free Kaggle GPUs (Qwen's full run took {{ f(F.resources.kaggle_hours, 1) }} GPU-hours). The code and analyses were developed with an AI coding assistant under our direction and review; the report includes a disclosure.

**Q30. What are the main limitations?**
(1) FlavorDB drift since 2018 (signs and ranks replicate; robustness analysis {{ pending("not yet run") }}). (2) LLM cuisines have {{ D.recipes_per_region }} recipes per region, so conclusions are region-level against equal-size genuine samples. (3) Roster: {% if F.pending_core %}{% for k in F.incomplete_models if F.models[k].tier == 'core' %}{{ F.models[k].name }} {{ F.models[k].status }}{% endfor %}{% else %}complete{% endif %}; scale contrast {{ pending("Qwen3.5-4B") }}. (4) Aliasing of LLM lines before curation. (5) Steering on existing samples, small per-region sizes, no CIs for critique-and-revise yet. (6) No human taste or authenticity rating.
